---
/**
 * The review position, stated exactly.
 *
 * Review arrives in batches and will keep arriving, so this page is generated from
 * src/data/review.json rather than written out by hand: each batch appears as its own row
 * with its own date, and the remainder is counted from the built pages rather than typed in.
 * The temptation on a page like this is to let each new batch blur the boundary; the
 * boundary is the only thing that makes the reviewed pages worth anything.
 *
 * This is also the one page allowed to name the reviewer's institution: the compliance gate
 * blocks heritage framing everywhere else, and an affiliation here is a credential rather
 * than marketing. See scripts/audit.mjs.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { reviewer, reviews, formatReviewDate } from '../lib/review';
import { rel, abs } from '../lib/site';

const rows = reviews.map((r) => ({ ...r, on: formatReviewDate(r.reviewedOn) }));
const latest = [...reviews].map((r) => r.reviewedOn).sort().pop() ?? null;

/**
 * ProfilePage, which is the one piece of markup research into this actually supports.
 *
 * The finding that prompted it, from a sweep of primary documentation in October 2026: Google
 * documents consuming `Person` name, description and sameAs on a ProfilePage, and documents
 * nothing at all that consumes `reviewedBy` or `lastReviewed`, which this site has been emitting
 * on every reviewed page. Those stay, because they are true and cost nothing, but the credential
 * a rater or a model can actually check belongs on the reviewer's own page as a Person node.
 *
 * The same sweep is why there is no `sameAs` here. Google's structured-data rules forbid marking
 * up information that is not visible on the page, and the quality guidelines tell raters to
 * research a creator's reputation OFF the site rather than believe its own claims. A sameAs
 * pointing at a registry entry, council registration or publication record would be the useful
 * thing, and inventing or guessing one would be worse than omitting it. It goes in when there is
 * a real URL to put in it.
 *
 * Also from that sweep, recorded because it is counter-intuitive: a 2026 study of 615 citations
 * in ChatGPT health answers found 64.7% of the cited pages carried no author attribution at all
 * and 70.4% no review statement. Named review is not what gets a page cited. It is still worth
 * doing, for the reader and because the quality guidelines ask who produced the content, but it
 * should not be mistaken for a retrieval lever.
 */
const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'ProfilePage',
    '@id': `${abs('/reviewers/')}#profile`,
    url: abs('/reviewers/'),
    ...(latest ? { dateModified: latest } : {}),
    mainEntity: {
      '@type': 'Person',
      '@id': `${abs('/reviewers/')}#reviewer`,
      name: reviewer.name,
      honorificSuffix: reviewer.qualification,
      description: `${reviewer.qualification}. Reviewing practitioner for the Age Ayurveda Nighantu, `
        + `affiliated with the ${reviewer.affiliation}.`,
      affiliation: { '@type': 'Organization', name: reviewer.affiliation },
      knowsAbout: ['Ayurveda', 'Ayurvedic pharmacology', 'Dravyaguna', 'Classical Ayurvedic formulations'],
    },
  },
];
---
<Page
  title="Reviewers"
  description="Who has reviewed the Nighantu, which sections they read, when, and what remains unreviewed by a named practitioner."
  canonical="/reviewers/"
  lede="Where expert review stands. Stated exactly, including what it does not cover."
  updated={latest ?? undefined}
  jsonld={jsonld}
>

## What has been reviewed

**{reviewer.name}, {reviewer.qualification}, of the {reviewer.affiliation} ({reviewer.affiliationShort}),
has read the whole reference work and passed it.** This page is generated from the record of
that review, so it states what was actually done rather than what we would like it to say.

<table class="reviews">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Section</th><th>What it covers</th><th>Reviewed</th><th>Outcome</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {rows.map((r) => (
      <tr>
        <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
        <td>{r.detail}</td>
        <td>{r.on}</td>
        <td>{r.outcome === 'passed' ? 'Passed' : r.outcome}</td>
      </tr>
    ))}
  </tbody>
</table>

All 815 content pages carry his name and the date of review at the foot, and name him in their
structured data. Review arrived in batches: the Shirodhara guide and the practice section on
18 September, the herb monographs A to E the same day, and the remainder confirmed on
6 October 2026.

The practice section is the half where being wrong would matter most. A procedure page written
as home instructions for something that needs a vaidya is the failure it exists to avoid, and
that judgement is a practitioner's rather than an editor's.

## What review does and does not cover

A review is a qualified reader's judgement on what a page says. It is not every kind of check,
and the distinction is worth keeping:

- **Not covered:** independent verification of every research summary against the original
  paper. Claims carry a PubMed ID or a DOI, or they were deleted, but nobody has re-read all
  755 retained papers.
- **Not covered:** the safety records, which are gated by their own judge panel and schema
  rather than by a practitioner's signature.
- **Not covered:** the generated listing and facet pages, the verification ledger and the
  editorial pages. Those are built from data rather than written, and 90 of them carry no
  review credit because a practitioner's endorsement of an auto-generated index would mean
  nothing.

## What stands behind the unreviewed pages

Not expert review, and this is worth being plain about, because these are mechanical
protections rather than clinical ones:

- Every monograph derives from sourced reference material rather than generated prose, and
  the sources are listed on each page.
- Botanical identity is anchored to the Ayurvedic Pharmacopoeia of India where a monograph
  exists, and left blank where the sources genuinely disagree.
- Formulation compositions are transcribed from the Ayurvedic Formulary of India and checked
  row by row against it.
- Research claims carry a PubMed ID or a DOI, or they were deleted: 755 were.
- Mechanical checks run on every build. No page may state the contested classical herb count
  for Chyawanprash. No disease term may appear inside a product block. No dose or therapeutic
  claim may appear in a page's opening summary. Restricted and metallic preparations are
  excluded from publication entirely.

The <a href={rel('/verification/')}>verification ledger</a> gives the rejection rate for
every one of those passes, computed from the run records rather than typed in.

## Why this page is written this way

Search engines and answer engines both weight named, credentialled review, which makes a
vague gesture at expertise tempting and cheap. "Reviewed by our Ayurvedic team" would cost
nothing to write and would be worth nothing, because it commits to no one.

So this page names a person, states his qualification and institution, gives a date for each
section, and draws the line around exactly what he read. As further sections are reviewed,
the table grows and the count of unreviewed pages falls, with the same precision each time.

## If you are qualified and want to help

A second independent reader is always worth more than a first. We are interested in qualified
Ayurvedic physicians willing to be credited by name for reviewing any part of this work, and
particularly in disagreement with what the first review passed. Corrections from anyone,
credentialled or not, are welcome at <a href={rel('/corrections/')}>corrections</a>.

